# WI-16 T1.4 — the guard's planted-defect pair

`t1-guard-absent-fixture.log` proved the guard fires when the fixture is absent.
That run happened *before* the fixture existed, so on its own it does not
distinguish the guard firing from the fixture simply never having been there.

This is the pair that does: point the constant at a slug that has never existed,
observe three failures each carrying the setup instruction, restore the constant,
observe three passes. The only thing that changes between the two runs is one
string in one file.

## Step 1 — plant the defect

`tests/integration/fixture.ts`, one line changed:

```diff
-export const FIXTURE_REPO = "manjula25/loop-integration-fixture";
+export const FIXTURE_REPO = "manjula25/loop-integration-fixture-does-not-exist";
```

## Step 2 — RED

```
$ npm run test:integration
 ❯ tests/integration/fixture.test.ts (3 tests | 3 failed) 17880ms
   ❯ the disposable integration fixture (WI-16 T1) (3)
     × exists on GitHub with the base branch the harness will drive 15840ms
     × carries a profile that is opted in, unnotifiable, and born green 1492ms
     × has a contract suite that is green in the production image 545ms

FixtureMissingError: integration fixture manjula25/loop-integration-fixture-does-not-exist is missing or unreachable (Command failed: gh repo view manjula25/loop-integration-fixture-does-not-exist --json name
GraphQL: Could not resolve to a Repository with the name 'manjula25/loop-integration-fixture-does-not-exist'. (repository)
). Provision it once, under owner authority, per docs/work/WI-16/implementation-plan.md T1.3. This test never creates it.

 Test Files  1 failed (1)
      Tests  3 failed (3)
   Duration  18.04s (tests 100%)
exit=1
```

All three tests fail, and each of the three carries the instruction — not one
failed suite with two skipped tests. That is the shape T1.2's restructure was for:
the guard runs inside every `it`, so the guard's own behaviour is what the count
reports.

The three failures also land at three different call sites (`fixture.test.ts:40`,
`:27` via `:51`, and `:32` via `:59`), which is the guard being reached by all
three tests rather than by a shared setup step.

## Step 3 — restore the constant

```
$ git diff --stat tests/integration/fixture.ts
(no output)
```

The file is byte-identical to its committed state. Run **before** the green run so
the green that follows cannot be attributed to any other edit.

## Step 4 — GREEN

```
$ npm run test:integration
 Test Files  1 passed (1)
      Tests  3 passed (3)
   Duration  34.86s (tests 100%)
exit=0
```

## What this pair does and does not prove

**Proves:** the guard is what fails the suite when the fixture is unreachable, and
the suite returns to green when it is reachable again. The check can fail, and it
can stop failing.

**Does not prove:** that the suite is green for the right reason. A test that
passed because it asserted nothing would look the same here. The green run's
individual assertions are exercised by §1 of `t1-fixture-green.log` (born-green in
the production image) and by the profile and branch assertions themselves, but
this pair is a check on the guard's *firing*, not on the suite's strength.

The two `describe`-level timings are also not comparable between runs — 18.04s
RED against 34.86s GREEN here — because the green run re-clones and re-runs the
container, and the red run short-circuits on `gh` before either happens. Neither
figure is a performance claim.